      "id": "01",
      "title": "RAII and stack unwinding",
      "question": "What is RAII, and why does it make code exception safe?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "R. A. I. I., question one. What is R. A. I. I., and why does it make code exception safe? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "What is <em>RAII</em>, and why is it exception safe?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "R. A. I. I. ties a resource to an object's lifetime. The constructor acquires it, and if that fails, it throws, so a half-built object never exists. The destructor releases it, and it must never throw.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Acquire in the constructor, <em>release in the destructor</em>",
            "file": "file_handler.hpp",
            "tag": "RAII",
            "size": 23,
            "width": 1100,
            "code": "class FileHandler {\npublic:\n    explicit FileHandler(const char* name) : file_(name) {\n        if (!file_) throw std::runtime_error(\"open failed\");\n    }\n    ~FileHandler() { file_.close(); }   // never throws\nprivate:\n    std::ofstream file_;\n};",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "ties",
                "lines": "1,8",
                "note": "The resource lives inside an object"
              },
              {
                "at": "acquires",
                "lines": "3",
                "note": "Acquire in the constructor"
              },
              {
                "at": "fails",
                "lines": "4",
                "note": "Failure throws: no half-built object"
              },
              {
                "at": "releases",
                "lines": "6",
                "note": "Release in the destructor, never throw"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
